WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.139
Research Ethics Committees can play a key role in promoting DEI.

2
00:00:07.489 --> 00:00:10.539
Here are four areas to look at when reviewing a proposal.

3
00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:13.423
First, representation and sampling.

4
00:00:13.773 --> 00:00:18.551
Are participant groups representative of the population affected by the research?

5
00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:23.189
Have barriers such as language, culture or accessibility been considered?

6
00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:26.691
Second, equity in design and implementation.

7
00:00:27.041 --> 00:00:29.773
Does the study minimise burdens of time, cost

8
00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:32.929
and travel on participants from marginalised groups?

9
00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:38.719
Are compensation and benefits fair and culturally appropriate, and are accommodations planned?

10
00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:41.731
Third, inclusion in team processes.

11
00:00:42.081 --> 00:00:45.662
Does the research team include varied perspectives relevant to

12
00:00:45.662 --> 00:00:49.185
the topic, and can all members contribute to decision-making?

13
00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:51.663
Fourth, ongoing monitoring.

14
00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:56.329
How will diversity and inclusion be tracked during the study, and how will

15
00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:02.103
findings and benefits be shared with all participant groups, including those historically excluded?

16
00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:07.749
These questions sit alongside the usual review of risk, consent and privacy,

17
00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:12.901
and help ensure fair treatment, benefit-sharing and respect for all populations.

